**Behaviour summary:**

| Route | Success | Errors |
|---|---|---|
| `POST /api/blogs` | 201 + blog | 400 (blank name) |
| `GET /api/blogs/{id}` | 200 | 404 |
| `PUT /api/blogs/{id}` | 200 + blog | 400, 404, 409 (concurrent edit) |
| `DELETE /api/blogs/{id}` | 204 (also deletes its posts) | 404 |
| `POST /api/blogs/{blogId}/posts` | 201 + post | 400, 404 (no blog) |
| `GET /api/posts/{id}` | 200 (increments `Views`) | 404 |
| `PUT /api/posts/{id}` | 200 + post (body: `blogId`, `title`) | 400, 404, 409 |
| `DELETE /api/posts/{id}` | 204 | 404 |

The code below is untested because I answered from the spec alone without building or running it.

Design choices:
- **Concurrency:** `Blog` and `Post` each have a `Version` concurrency token. A PUT that loses a race returns 409 instead of silently overwriting.
- **Views:** `GET /api/posts/{id}` increments `Views` with one atomic `UPDATE`, so concurrent reads don't lose counts. Clients can't set `Views`.
- **Deletes:** deleting a blog removes its posts in the same transaction.
- **Retries:** PUT and DELETE are safe to repeat. A retried POST creates a duplicate; fixing that would need a client-supplied id or an idempotency key.

