Design notes:
- **Retries:** `DELETE` returns 204 even when the ticket is already gone, so a retry after a timeout is harmless. `PUT` is a full replacement, so repeating it is safe. `POST` accepts an optional `Idempotency-Key` header; a repeated key returns the originally created ticket instead of creating a duplicate.
- **Concurrency:** `Version` is an EF concurrency token, incremented on every update. A conflicting `PUT` is reloaded and reapplied up to three times (last writer wins, since the body is a full replacement), then returns 409.
- **View tracking:** `GET` increments `ViewCount` and sets `LastViewedAt` with a single atomic `ExecuteUpdate`, so concurrent reads don't lose counts. Clients can't set these fields because `TicketInput` doesn't include them.
- **Validation:** `Subject` and `Category` are required, and `[ApiController]` returns 400 on invalid bodies.
- **Untested:** I did not compile or run this code. Note that `:memory:` SQLite connection strings give each pooled connection its own database, so use a file-based connection string with `EnsureCreated()`.

The implementation is below as three files. Ids are server-generated GUIDs, and `ViewCount` and `LastViewedAt` are only changed by the server.

- **Retries:** `POST` accepts an optional `Idempotency-Key` header. A retried request with the same key returns the ticket created by the first attempt instead of creating a duplicate. `DELETE` returns 204 whether or not the ticket exists, so retries are safe.
- **Concurrent edits:** every ticket has a `Version` that is returned as an `ETag`. `PUT` accepts an optional `If-Match` header and returns 412 if the version is stale. If two writes race, the loser gets 409 (or 412 when `If-Match` was sent).
- **Views:** `GET` increments `ViewCount` and sets `LastViewedAt` in a single atomic SQL update, so concurrent reads don't lose counts.
- **Field protection:** `PUT` and `POST` bind only `subject`, `priority`, `escalated` and `category`, so clients cannot set the server-maintained fields.

