Design notes:

- `POST` accepts a client-supplied `id`; if it already exists with identical content the existing resource is returned (`200`), so retries after timeouts don't create duplicates. A conflicting reuse of the same `id` returns `409`.
- Updates use `ExecuteUpdateAsync`/`ExecuteDeleteAsync` — single atomic SQL statements, so concurrent edits resolve as last-write-wins without lost-update exceptions or stale tracked entities.
- `GET /api/warehouses/{id}` stamps `LastAuditAt` (UTC) and `GET /api/shipments/{id}` increments `DownloadCount`, both atomically in the database. Clients cannot set either field — the request DTOs don't include them.
- `LastAuditAt`, `DownloadCount`, `DownloadCount`, and `DepotId`/`Consignment` naming are internal; the API exposes `warehouseId`, `lastAuditAt`, and `downloadCount` in camelCase JSON.
- SQLite `BUSY`/`LOCKED` errors are retried with backoff via `DbRetry`, and warehouse deletion removes its shipments inside a transaction regardless of FK enforcement.

I'll provide the implementation as several C# files compiled into one assembly.

