Design notes:

- **Entities**: `Depot` and `Consignment` are named exactly as required; `Consignment.DownloadCount` and `Depot.LastAuditAt` are server-maintained only — request DTOs don't expose them, so clients can't set them.
- **Idempotent creates**: POST bodies accept an optional `id`. Retried creates with the same id and same payload return the existing record (201 with same resource); a conflicting payload returns 409. Races are handled by catching `DbUpdateException` and re-reading.
- **DownloadCount**: incremented on every `GET /api/shipments/{id}`.
- **LastAuditAt**: stamped on warehouse PUT and DELETE (audit-relevant mutations).
- **Concurrency**: EF Core concurrency exceptions on PUT/DELETE map to 409; the GET counter increment retries by reloading rather than failing.
- **Serialization**: default ASP.NET Core camelCase JSON handles `capacityCubicMeters`, `warehouseId`, `weightKg`, etc. automatically. GUIDs round-trip as strings.
- **Decimal storage**: `WeightKg` uses `HasColumnType("TEXT")` so SQLite stores the decimal exactly without floating-point drift; EF reads it back losslessly.

One caveat worth flagging: whether `DownloadCount` should increment on GET is inferred ("download" = read), and whether `LastAuditAt` should update on every mutation vs. only some events is ambiguous — I chose the most defensible interpretation.

